WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.099 --> 00:00:03.030
Let us then turn to the Chief Executive’s
report.

00:00:03.030 --> 00:00:04.060
David Nicholson: Thank you.

00:00:04.060 --> 00:00:09.680
I would just like to talk a bit about the
changes around 1st April and the implications

00:00:09.680 --> 00:00:11.540
of those for all of us.

00:00:11.540 --> 00:00:20.970
I want to talk a bit about the response of
both the government and NHS England to the

00:00:20.970 --> 00:00:25.039
Francis Report; we had I think a really good
discussion at our last meeting around all

00:00:25.039 --> 00:00:26.039
of that.

00:00:26.039 --> 00:00:30.380
I also want to talk about a couple of issues
that I think are very significant in the sense

00:00:30.380 --> 00:00:37.499
that they show the move between being an organisation
in setup to one that is actually taking operational

00:00:37.499 --> 00:00:38.499
responsibility.

00:00:38.499 --> 00:00:48.120
I want to cover 111, and I want to cover the
Leeds Children’s Heart Centre.

00:00:48.120 --> 00:00:52.909
Change of name but not responsibilities.

00:00:52.909 --> 00:00:59.120
The Executive Team overall took the commitment
that we would visit all our staff and meet

00:00:59.120 --> 00:01:02.659
with all our staff over April, and we are
doing that at the moment; we are going to

00:01:02.659 --> 00:01:04.119
visit each of the area teams.

00:01:04.119 --> 00:01:10.619
I have done five of them so far, and we have
literally met hundreds of staff to talk about

00:01:10.619 --> 00:01:20.230
the NHS England, what we are trying to do,
the business plan, our mission, vision and

00:01:20.230 --> 00:01:22.549
values; all of those things that you would
expect us to do.

00:01:22.549 --> 00:01:27.719
They have been really, really good meetings
so far; we have a fantastic group of staff

00:01:27.719 --> 00:01:32.250
out there who are really keen to take services
forward.

00:01:32.250 --> 00:01:39.490
The issue of the name change has been really
well received by staff, and I think it is

00:01:39.490 --> 00:01:43.280
an important part of it to set out.

00:01:43.280 --> 00:01:48.490
Sometimes people misunderstand what the NHS
is in the sense that they think it is just

00:01:48.490 --> 00:01:54.899
a set of organisations that work together
through contracts and relationships and all

00:01:54.899 --> 00:01:55.899
of that.

00:01:55.899 --> 00:02:01.299
Actually, it is the people who work within
it; and the thing that binds everyone together

00:02:01.299 --> 00:02:08.750
is the values and principles the people hold,
whether that be around the importance of putting

00:02:08.750 --> 00:02:15.160
patients at the centre of what we do, or around
Everyone Counts, that not only are we responsible

00:02:15.160 --> 00:02:24.240
for delivering services to patients, but also
that we need to make sure that for those people

00:02:24.240 --> 00:02:28.850
who have difficulty connecting with the NHS
for a whole variety of reasons, maybe not

00:02:28.850 --> 00:02:35.000
their own fault, we make extra effort to make
sure that we support and help them with their

00:02:35.000 --> 00:02:37.200
health and healthcare.

00:02:37.200 --> 00:02:43.440
Those values and principles are enshrined
in the NHS constitution, and of course, one

00:02:43.440 --> 00:02:49.300
of our main responsibilities is the promotion
of that NHS constitution widely, both in the

00:02:49.300 --> 00:02:52.850
NHS and beyond in society.

00:02:52.850 --> 00:02:59.450
People genuinely believe that the name connects
that in a much better, more accessible way

00:02:59.450 --> 00:03:05.730
to people as we go forward, and I think certainly
if you talk to our staff around, you can see

00:03:05.730 --> 00:03:08.540
how committed they are to that kind of work.

00:03:08.540 --> 00:03:14.770
As I say, I think we have a really great bunch
of staff, and we are going to continue to

00:03:14.770 --> 00:03:16.380
do that as we go forward.

00:03:16.380 --> 00:03:21.790
An important part of what happened toward
the end of the last financial year, as the

00:03:21.790 --> 00:03:26.640
old organisations were closing down, was the
publication of the government’s response

00:03:26.640 --> 00:03:27.640
to Francis.

00:03:27.640 --> 00:03:38.010
As part of that, all of the constituent organisations
around the NHS, both regulatory and management

00:03:38.010 --> 00:03:43.370
and commissioning, signed a statement of common
purpose.

00:03:43.370 --> 00:03:51.640
One of the most important lessons, in a sense,
from the past, is how organisations work together

00:03:51.640 --> 00:03:54.920
for the benefit of patients, and that enshrines
that in that.

00:03:54.920 --> 00:04:00.490
We were engaged and involved in conversations
around the government’s response.

00:04:00.490 --> 00:04:07.800
It is really important as we get into implementation
of changes that need to be made that we continue

00:04:07.800 --> 00:04:09.320
to work collectively together.

00:04:09.320 --> 00:04:16.049
It is what our communities and patients expect.

00:04:16.049 --> 00:04:21.740
NHS England has a major part to play in all
of that.

00:04:21.740 --> 00:04:27.430
We heralded quite a lot of that in our planning
document ‘Everyone Counts’, which set

00:04:27.430 --> 00:04:32.750
out I think a set of really important objectives
that we want to deliver.

00:04:32.750 --> 00:04:39.640
It is reflected in the business plan, which
we are going to discuss a little later.

00:04:39.640 --> 00:04:46.350
I would just particularly say that obviously
inspection and all the sorts of things that

00:04:46.350 --> 00:04:51.640
are in the government’s response are really
important and really key to take forward.

00:04:51.640 --> 00:04:58.580
I think our particular response, our particular
role in all of this, is on the one hand to

00:04:58.580 --> 00:05:06.930
inspire and motivate the people who work within
the NHS, and I think the C&amp;O strategy and

00:05:06.930 --> 00:05:09.710
the work around that is a really important
part of that.

00:05:09.710 --> 00:05:14.040
We are sponsoring that and taking that forward;
it is set out in the business plan.

00:05:14.040 --> 00:05:19.000
Also, giving patients more power, more clout,
in the service, which again, is reflected

00:05:19.000 --> 00:05:20.190
in the business plan.

00:05:20.190 --> 00:05:29.840
I think in those ways we will make our big
contribution to taking the NHS forward.

00:05:29.840 --> 00:05:34.100
It was always going to be a challenging time
for us, I think, when we moved from setup

00:05:34.100 --> 00:05:36.980
to operation.

00:05:36.980 --> 00:05:42.770
If you think about the scale of the operation
that we are responsible for (or the commissioning

00:05:42.770 --> 00:05:46.470
of that), it is very significant.

00:05:46.470 --> 00:05:50.070
I just want to identify a couple of things
that I think have happened relatively early

00:05:50.070 --> 00:05:51.070
on.

00:05:51.070 --> 00:05:57.770
The first one is we took on operational responsibility
for 111, which had been planned for some time.

00:05:57.770 --> 00:06:04.550
There is no doubt that there had been some
significant operational problems in putting

00:06:04.550 --> 00:06:07.870
111 into place.

00:06:07.870 --> 00:06:15.740
We have had serious difficulties in relation
to people being able to get through, just

00:06:15.740 --> 00:06:19.990
simply in terms of call handling and the phone
arrangements, and in some places, we have

00:06:19.990 --> 00:06:26.440
had a large number of people who have had
to give up and try again later.

00:06:26.440 --> 00:06:28.480
That is clearly unacceptable.

00:06:28.480 --> 00:06:32.440
We have let some of our patients down, and
we need to think really carefully both about

00:06:32.440 --> 00:06:36.730
the lessons we learn about how we do that,
but also, more importantly at the moment,

00:06:36.730 --> 00:06:38.881
to get the service up and running.

00:06:38.881 --> 00:06:45.860
We took decisive action at the beginning of
April, led by Barbara and her team, putting

00:06:45.860 --> 00:06:54.070
into place a set of contingencies that we
had available.

00:06:54.070 --> 00:06:58.870
They continue to operate, and we can see that
the service is improving at the moment (we

00:06:58.870 --> 00:07:04.800
are monitoring on a daily basis), with one
or two areas where we still have issues to

00:07:04.800 --> 00:07:05.800
deal with.

00:07:05.800 --> 00:07:09.570
We need to get that stable, and we are working
really hard to make that a reality.

00:07:09.570 --> 00:07:15.669
As I say, performance is improving on a daily
basis.

00:07:15.669 --> 00:07:23.990
We also need to reflect I think, in time,
on what we should do around some of the failures

00:07:23.990 --> 00:07:27.420
that we have had, particularly around the
providers of service, which have simply not

00:07:27.420 --> 00:07:29.830
delivered what was expected.

00:07:29.830 --> 00:07:32.520
We need to make decisions about what we do
with that.

00:07:32.520 --> 00:07:37.760
I think we need to think more long term about
what kind of service we really want for our

00:07:37.760 --> 00:07:43.289
patients, going forward, as well as having
a pretty good reflection, a deep reflection,

00:07:43.289 --> 00:07:48.790
on what we could have done better to make
it a more successful outcome for patients.

00:07:48.790 --> 00:07:53.610
All of that is being taken forward, and we
will bring all of that to Board meetings in

00:07:53.610 --> 00:07:56.330
the future.

00:07:56.330 --> 00:08:04.400
The second area is around the Leeds Children’s
Heart Unit, and the work that we have done

00:08:04.400 --> 00:08:07.260
around all of that.

00:08:07.260 --> 00:08:14.310
I would personally commend the work that Sir
Bruce did when faced with a set of information;

00:08:14.310 --> 00:08:19.080
there was some data, but also some what I
think might be described as soft intelligence

00:08:19.080 --> 00:08:21.860
around what was happening in the Unit.

00:08:21.860 --> 00:08:26.690
What Bruce did in those circumstances was
meet the Unit, meet the people who ran the

00:08:26.690 --> 00:08:32.339
hospital, in a sense hold a mirror up to those
people about what was happening; and they

00:08:32.339 --> 00:08:40.029
agreed to suspend operations during the next
period.

00:08:40.029 --> 00:08:46.980
We were then able to put into place the process
that had been identified by the National Quality

00:08:46.980 --> 00:08:48.079
Board.

00:08:48.079 --> 00:08:55.149
One of the sets of processes that came out
of the original Francis report about what

00:08:55.149 --> 00:09:00.050
you do when you have intelligence that something
might not be quite right in an organisation,

00:09:00.050 --> 00:09:03.460
and patient safety may be at risk.

00:09:03.460 --> 00:09:08.430
We put in a group of people to investigate
what was happening.

00:09:08.430 --> 00:09:14.260
We also set up a series of risk summits involving
all of the organisations, to enable us to

00:09:14.260 --> 00:09:21.319
make sure that at the time when it was right
to reintroduce surgery, it was done in the

00:09:21.319 --> 00:09:22.689
safest and most organised way.

00:09:22.689 --> 00:09:27.949
I think that is what is essentially happening.

00:09:27.949 --> 00:09:34.191
Throughout all of that, our responsibility
as NHS England is absolutely clearly to put

00:09:34.191 --> 00:09:41.790
ourselves on the side of safety and the children,
and I think that is what has happened over

00:09:41.790 --> 00:09:42.790
the period.

00:09:42.790 --> 00:09:48.939
We are not by any stretch of the imagine at
the end of this story – there is still quite

00:09:48.939 --> 00:09:56.010
a lot of work to be done by the Leeds Children’s
Heart Centre to improve what they do and to

00:09:56.010 --> 00:10:01.270
give both themselves as an organisation and
the wider community assurance of what they

00:10:01.270 --> 00:10:06.509
are doing – but we are satisfied that we
are in the right place now in terms of taking

00:10:06.509 --> 00:10:10.119
that forward and that it was the right thing
to do to reintroduce surgery.

00:10:10.119 --> 00:10:15.910
Those were two big operational issues that
we tackled, and I am sure it is the beginning

00:10:15.910 --> 00:10:16.910
of many.

00:10:16.910 --> 00:10:22.269
Again, I think we should take the opportunity
to stand back at some time and reflect on

00:10:22.269 --> 00:10:29.579
what we could have done better, what lessons
we learn as an organisation as we take this

00:10:29.579 --> 00:10:31.800
great responsibility that we have forward.

00:10:31.800 --> 00:10:33.149
Malcolm Grant: Thank you, David.

00:10:33.149 --> 00:10:38.889
I am absolutely clear that that is right and
we have to do a very serious appraisal of

00:10:38.889 --> 00:10:43.679
both 111 and Leeds in terms of lessons learnt
for NHS England.

00:10:43.679 --> 00:10:49.779
If I may, I would like to ask Bruce Keogh,
our National Medical Director, to perhaps

00:10:49.779 --> 00:10:52.300
say a little bit more about the Leeds experience.

00:10:52.300 --> 00:10:53.300
Bruce Keogh: Thank you, Chair.

00:10:53.300 --> 00:10:57.470
If I could just put a little context around
this.

00:10:57.470 --> 00:11:02.610
Children’s heart surgery is some of the
most technically and intellectually demanding

00:11:02.610 --> 00:11:05.079
surgery there is.

00:11:05.079 --> 00:11:11.029
There are new operations being performed now
that did not exist 20 years ago.

00:11:11.029 --> 00:11:17.320
We have babies that are getting much smaller
because we are operating earlier on them.

00:11:17.320 --> 00:11:21.350
The post-operative care as a result is much
more complicated than it used to be, both

00:11:21.350 --> 00:11:24.519
in the intensive care unit and in the ward.

00:11:24.519 --> 00:11:30.850
Both professional and public expectations
of good results are growing.

00:11:30.850 --> 00:11:38.019
That has resulted, I think, in widespread
agreement that it makes sense to concentrate

00:11:38.019 --> 00:11:41.360
expertise in fewer units.

00:11:41.360 --> 00:11:47.319
We believe that we should do that in the pursuit
of excellence and in the pursuit of future-proofing

00:11:47.319 --> 00:11:52.129
an increasingly complex medical and surgical
specialty.

00:11:52.129 --> 00:11:56.439
That gave rise to a programme called the Safe
and Sustainable programme, from which – I

00:11:56.439 --> 00:12:02.510
need to be absolutely clear – I have been
utterly independent since its inception.

00:12:02.510 --> 00:12:09.999
Two weeks ago, I was confronted with a number
of issues that coalesced.

00:12:09.999 --> 00:12:16.139
Firstly, a surgeon had been taken off surgical
duties in Leeds.

00:12:16.139 --> 00:12:23.910
Secondly, most of the operating was being
conducted by locum surgeons because the senior

00:12:23.910 --> 00:12:28.920
surgeon in Leeds was away on holiday, and
there was a long weekend approaching with

00:12:28.920 --> 00:12:31.230
Easter.

00:12:31.230 --> 00:12:37.309
In addition, I was approached by a surgeon
from Newcastle who reiterated some concerns

00:12:37.309 --> 00:12:40.459
that had been previously been expressed by
the Children’s Heart Federation, in which

00:12:40.459 --> 00:12:45.399
they had communicated with the Care Quality
Commission that they felt there were issues

00:12:45.399 --> 00:12:52.119
around the way patients attending the service
in Leeds were given access to choice of where

00:12:52.119 --> 00:12:58.790
they might go, and questioning the quality
of advice that some of them had received.

00:12:58.790 --> 00:13:03.970
The final thing that happened was that Sir
Roger Boyle, previously a highly successful

00:13:03.970 --> 00:13:09.390
National Clinical Director for cardiovascular
disease over the last 12 or so years, sent

00:13:09.390 --> 00:13:17.800
me some risk-adjusted data – albeit early
– that showed that the mortality rate in

00:13:17.800 --> 00:13:25.050
Leeds was 2.75 times the national average,
and that there was clear blue water between

00:13:25.050 --> 00:13:31.300
Leeds and the other units, that the mortality
rate was rising.

00:13:31.300 --> 00:13:39.379
To my mind, that was the final thing that
made me think we needed to do something.

00:13:39.379 --> 00:13:44.399
The irony of it was that this information
all coalesced in the same week that the government

00:13:44.399 --> 00:13:47.379
responded to the Francis Inquiry.

00:13:47.379 --> 00:13:51.769
One of the things that the Francis Inquiry
had in common with the Bristol Royal Infirmary

00:13:51.769 --> 00:13:58.749
Inquiry into children’s heart surgery some
13 years ago was that when confronted with

00:13:58.749 --> 00:14:05.549
data, people argued and dithered over the
meaning of the data and its utility.

00:14:05.549 --> 00:14:10.069
Meantime, patients were being palmed somewhere
else.

00:14:10.069 --> 00:14:14.619
It was against that background that I thought
our options were limited.

00:14:14.619 --> 00:14:18.449
I and colleagues met with the Trust.

00:14:18.449 --> 00:14:24.009
As David Nicholson says, we held up a mirror
to the Trust, and I think everybody agreed

00:14:24.009 --> 00:14:30.129
that a sensible approach would be to pause;
not close the Unit, simply to pause surgery

00:14:30.129 --> 00:14:31.249
in that Unit.

00:14:31.249 --> 00:14:37.629
It is my belief that if we had not done so
and something had happened over that long

00:14:37.629 --> 00:14:43.350
weekend, we would be having a much more difficult
conversation than we are having at the moment.

00:14:43.350 --> 00:14:49.379
This pause was in the interests of the patients,
of the families of those children in Leeds,

00:14:49.379 --> 00:14:54.019
of the surgeons, and of the Trust.

00:14:54.019 --> 00:14:56.230
There were two steps to what we undertook.

00:14:56.230 --> 00:15:03.230
The first was, we needed to review the mortality
data to check its veracity and validity.

00:15:03.230 --> 00:15:07.199
Secondly, we needed to review the Unit.

00:15:07.199 --> 00:15:12.869
In the process of reviewing the mortality
data, it became absolutely clear that Leeds

00:15:12.869 --> 00:15:16.129
had submitted incomplete and inadequate data.

00:15:16.129 --> 00:15:22.040
It is my view – and I need to be clear about
this – that people undertaking surgical

00:15:22.040 --> 00:15:26.620
and other procedures in our NHS have a duty
to be able to describe what they do and define

00:15:26.620 --> 00:15:28.389
how well they do it.

00:15:28.389 --> 00:15:31.800
Otherwise, I think they forfeit some of their
professionalism.

00:15:31.800 --> 00:15:36.879
This applies both to individuals and to organisations.

00:15:36.879 --> 00:15:39.040
Leeds had submitted inadequate data.

00:15:39.040 --> 00:15:47.119
To give you an example, you need the weight
of a baby to be able to work out how risky

00:15:47.119 --> 00:15:54.149
the operation is, in taking account of case-mix
in the analysis.

00:15:54.149 --> 00:15:59.790
The weight of the baby was missing in a substantial
number of records submitted by Leeds.

00:15:59.790 --> 00:16:08.019
In fact, that missing data was 20 times worse
than any other unit in the country.

00:16:08.019 --> 00:16:12.379
We corrected all of that data, and it was
submitted and reanalysed.

00:16:12.379 --> 00:16:20.439
I am glad to say that once things had been
reanalysed, Leeds came back into the envelope

00:16:20.439 --> 00:16:22.679
of a normal national mortality rate.

00:16:22.679 --> 00:16:27.899
It is against that background that we have
been able to satisfy ourselves that the immediate

00:16:27.899 --> 00:16:45.220
issues 

00:16:45.220 --> 00:16:52.160
of [break in audio] this surgery was undertaken.

00:16:52.160 --> 00:17:00.699
They took a view that what they had found,
combined with the newly submitted and reanalysed

00:17:00.699 --> 00:17:04.980
data, meant that it was reasonable to recommence
surgery.

00:17:04.980 --> 00:17:09.689
All of these issues were discussed at a risk
summit, which is part of the process that

00:17:09.689 --> 00:17:15.459
has been defined by the National Quality Board,
where NHS England, the Care Quality Commission,

00:17:15.459 --> 00:17:21.230
the Trust Development Agency and the Hospital,
were all represented.

00:17:21.230 --> 00:17:25.470
It was at that risk summit that the agreement
was taken to recommence surgery.

00:17:25.470 --> 00:17:30.270
However, I should emphasise that there are
still some unresolved issues that remain,

00:17:30.270 --> 00:17:33.150
which do not have the same level of urgency.

00:17:33.150 --> 00:17:38.880
First of all, the Children’s Heart Federation
in Newcastle have raised issues about referral

00:17:38.880 --> 00:17:42.570
and access to other units.

00:17:42.570 --> 00:17:47.049
When surgery is very, very complicated, there
are some units that have better expertise

00:17:47.049 --> 00:17:51.490
at performing certain procedures than others,
and that is normal.

00:17:51.490 --> 00:17:57.799
Referral for different cases to different
units is normal in all aspects of surgery.

00:17:57.799 --> 00:18:02.691
Secondly, we are looking at how complaints
had been dealt with by the Trust, and there

00:18:02.691 --> 00:18:07.230
is a case-note review going on where there
are issues of concerns about treatment of

00:18:07.230 --> 00:18:09.529
children in the Trust.

00:18:09.529 --> 00:18:14.320
There is a further risk summit to take place
in the near future, in a week or so, where

00:18:14.320 --> 00:18:19.220
these additional issues will be considered
in the light of further emerging information.

00:18:19.220 --> 00:18:20.220
Thank you, Chair.

00:18:20.220 --> 00:18:22.660
Malcolm Grant: Bruce, thank you for that explanation.

00:18:22.660 --> 00:18:27.260
May I invite members of the Board to raise
any issues or questions, both on David’s

00:18:27.260 --> 00:18:29.480
report and on Bruce’s addition.

00:18:29.480 --> 00:18:38.980
Naguib Kheraj: On both of these issues, I
think it would be really helpful to be clear

00:18:38.980 --> 00:18:46.460
about the scope of our responsibility, as
NHS England.

00:18:46.460 --> 00:18:50.529
On the 111 service, do we run everything?

00:18:50.529 --> 00:18:56.039
Is it staffed by people who work for NHS England,
managed by us?

00:18:56.039 --> 00:19:03.039
Is any element of it outsourced or is it something
that we are totally responsible for across

00:19:03.039 --> 00:19:04.630
the country?

00:19:04.630 --> 00:19:13.149
On the Leeds thing, when you were involved
in the decision, Bruce, was that in the context

00:19:13.149 --> 00:19:22.399
of your old role as Medical Director, or was
it as NHS England?

00:19:22.399 --> 00:19:32.080
In making the decision to suspend operations,
do we do that because it is a specialist service

00:19:32.080 --> 00:19:38.559
that we are commissioning directly, or is
it because you are using some other instrument

00:19:38.559 --> 00:19:47.610
or lever in either your [break in audio]?

00:19:47.610 --> 00:20:04.159
Ed Smith: David talks about lessons learned,
and I 

00:20:04.159 --> 00:20:14.570
think it is really important that we learn
lessons, but balanced with those lessons learned,

00:20:14.570 --> 00:20:24.010
I think there is being clear about accountability.

00:20:24.010 --> 00:20:30.610
That is not in a witch-hunt sense, but we
have to be clear that we will hold providers

00:20:30.610 --> 00:20:36.590
and suppliers to us accountable, and we will
hold ourselves and our people accountable

00:20:36.590 --> 00:20:40.900
for the responsibilities and obligations that
we have.

00:20:40.900 --> 00:20:49.419
I think that is a key part of learning lessons,
but holding accountability as part of the

00:20:49.419 --> 00:20:50.419
system.

00:20:50.419 --> 00:20:55.009
Malcolm Grant: Before you respond, David,
Victor?

00:20:55.009 --> 00:20:59.200
Victor Adebowale: Two slightly different points.

00:20:59.200 --> 00:21:01.250
One was on the constitution.

00:21:01.250 --> 00:21:05.029
Malcolm Grant: In which case, may I take responses?

00:21:05.029 --> 00:21:07.860
Moira, is yours on Leeds?

00:21:07.860 --> 00:21:11.100
Moira Gibb: Again, I think the public do not
have very much information about the whole

00:21:11.100 --> 00:21:12.899
111 planning, and probably did not pay very
much attention.

00:21:12.899 --> 00:21:18.120
If I count myself as a member of the public,
I did not pay much attention to the rationale

00:21:18.120 --> 00:21:23.500
behind it, the financial savings that are
intended to emerge from it.

00:21:23.500 --> 00:21:28.520
I just wonder again if David could address
the issue of who should be doing that explaining.

00:21:28.520 --> 00:21:30.009
Is that our responsibility?

00:21:30.009 --> 00:21:33.529
Malcolm Grant: Okay; just pick up Margaret’s
point.

00:21:33.529 --> 00:21:41.500
Margaret Casely-Hayford: Yes, because it is
tangential to Moira’s point, that is, that

00:21:41.500 --> 00:21:50.090
if we are looking at the responsibility on
111, the contingency arrangements for the

00:21:50.090 --> 00:22:03.659
cutover need to be very clearly covered within
the business planning of the responsible entities.

00:22:03.659 --> 00:22:10.700
That is something that I am not sure has actually
been articulated as clearly as I would like

00:22:10.700 --> 00:22:11.750
to see.

00:22:11.750 --> 00:22:23.270
Obviously, it is that cutover that is the
vulnerable element, if 111 fails because it

00:22:23.270 --> 00:22:24.270
is new, that is one aspect.

00:22:24.270 --> 00:22:27.830
If the existing arrangement still subsists
and one needs to make business plan provision

00:22:27.830 --> 00:22:35.450
for that subsisting cover, that is another
element, and that needs to be clearly covered

00:22:35.450 --> 00:22:36.450
and dealt with.

00:22:36.450 --> 00:22:37.840
Chairman, I do have another point.

00:22:37.840 --> 00:22:41.580
Malcolm Grant: I would quite like to take
together all the points about 111 – and

00:22:41.580 --> 00:22:46.299
Barbara may wish to come in as well – and
then separately, the points about Leeds.

00:22:46.299 --> 00:22:48.320
Then we may move to other points.

00:22:48.320 --> 00:22:50.380
David Nicholson: I am sure she will.

00:22:50.380 --> 00:22:55.960
The complexity of the 111 position is, of
course, we moved systems halfway; statutorily,

00:22:55.960 --> 00:23:00.419
organisations moved during that period.

00:23:00.419 --> 00:23:10.559
The original idea behind 111 was that the
urgent care system was becoming more and more

00:23:10.559 --> 00:23:27.159
complex, that it was more and more difficult
to make sure that the patient got to the right

00:23:27.159 --> 00:23:31.590
place as fast as possible as things like minor
injuries units, urgent care centres, out-of-hours

00:23:31.590 --> 00:23:35.149
services, a whole plethora of things were
becoming available to patients.

00:23:35.149 --> 00:23:37.500
The idea behind 111 was that it was one telephone
where people could ring up and they would

00:23:37.500 --> 00:23:43.659
be directed to the right place in the system
as fast as possible, so they could access

00:23:43.659 --> 00:23:48.080
that service really rapidly.

00:23:48.080 --> 00:23:57.509
The responsibilities for that were originally
given to the PCTs to do, so they are commissioned

00:23:57.509 --> 00:23:59.510
in a whole variety of different ways.

00:23:59.510 --> 00:24:03.919
They have been transferred over to the Clinical
Commissioning Groups.

00:24:03.919 --> 00:24:13.470
There are 46 different contracts being let
on 111 across the whole country.

00:24:13.470 --> 00:24:14.929
Those contracts were led locally.

00:24:14.929 --> 00:24:17.340
In a sense, that is what we have inherited.

00:24:17.340 --> 00:24:22.460
We are responsible for the oversight of that,
not for the commissioning; we do not do commissioning

00:24:22.460 --> 00:24:23.460
directly ourselves.

00:24:23.460 --> 00:24:33.769
It has been commissioned in those places using
a variety of providers, from NHS to the private

00:24:33.769 --> 00:24:38.280
sector to the independent sector; so there
is a whole variety of providers as well.

00:24:38.280 --> 00:24:42.850
I think this is one of the lessons we are
going to have to think about when we take

00:24:42.850 --> 00:24:44.500
all of that forward.

00:24:44.500 --> 00:24:49.200
Our role in those circumstances is the oversight
of it, to make sure it is put in place.

00:24:49.200 --> 00:24:50.200
Malcolm Grant: Barbara?

00:24:50.200 --> 00:24:51.440
Barbara Hakin: Yes, thank you.

00:24:51.440 --> 00:24:56.990
In answer to your question, we certainly do
not employ the staff, because it is a service

00:24:56.990 --> 00:24:58.070
that CCGs commission.

00:24:58.070 --> 00:25:00.760
As David said, there is a range of providers.

00:25:00.760 --> 00:25:07.200
The commissioning of the service transferred
over the bank holiday weekend from PCTs to

00:25:07.200 --> 00:25:13.540
CCGs, and as David said, what was apparent
is that patients were not getting a satisfactory

00:25:13.540 --> 00:25:19.950
level of service from this service, which
was being rolled out across the country, with

00:25:19.950 --> 00:25:24.280
different sites going live and different sites
taking on more responsibility at different

00:25:24.280 --> 00:25:25.280
times.

00:25:25.280 --> 00:25:31.220
At that point, we believed that our role was
to support CCGs as commissioners, because

00:25:31.220 --> 00:25:36.590
I think NHS England’s role in supporting
all CCGs across the country to commission

00:25:36.590 --> 00:25:40.799
services, to be the best they can be for their
population, is really important.

00:25:40.799 --> 00:25:46.659
I think given the magnitude of the issue and
the concerns we had about it, it was right

00:25:46.659 --> 00:25:52.920
that we stepped in to support them, and I
think that support from CCGs was hugely welcomed.

00:25:52.920 --> 00:26:00.320
In the run-up to Easter, I set up a national
group to give oversight and support to the

00:26:00.320 --> 00:26:02.190
CCGs in this commissioning.

00:26:02.190 --> 00:26:06.659
There is no doubt that in many instances,
it was about providers failing to deliver

00:26:06.659 --> 00:26:10.669
the service that had been commissioned, and
there were issues with the number of staff

00:26:10.669 --> 00:26:12.640
that providers had secured.

00:26:12.640 --> 00:26:19.240
As David said, there was a range of providers,
some of them NHS Trust, some of them not.

00:26:19.240 --> 00:26:24.440
I felt that in our role as commissioners,
we need to do a broad range of things to secure

00:26:24.440 --> 00:26:25.559
the service.

00:26:25.559 --> 00:26:31.370
Part of that was holding the providers to
account, and robust discussions went on both

00:26:31.370 --> 00:26:38.240
at local level – what with the CCG leaders
and our area team directors talking to the

00:26:38.240 --> 00:26:42.309
providers from the 46 sites – and also at
national level.

00:26:42.309 --> 00:26:47.280
On a number of occasions, there was one fairly
large organisation that had several sites,

00:26:47.280 --> 00:26:51.059
so there were discussions at national level
between me and the leaders of those organisations

00:26:51.059 --> 00:26:55.710
about how the providers could deliver what
they had agreed to deliver.

00:26:55.710 --> 00:27:00.929
We also felt that in our role as commissioners,
it was important to do the other things to

00:27:00.929 --> 00:27:05.779
put around those providers in order to secure
the service for patients.

00:27:05.779 --> 00:27:11.529
That was about bringing in contingencies;
in some instances, where it was possible,

00:27:11.529 --> 00:27:30.070
using other providers to help with the call-handling;
in some instances, where [break in audio]

00:27:30.070 --> 00:27:35.370
to take the pressure off the new 111 system
whilst they got up to speed and actually got

00:27:35.370 --> 00:27:37.240
their call handlers in place.

00:27:37.240 --> 00:27:44.070
I think that is what I saw, Naguib, as our
role as the supporters of CCGs in the commissioning

00:27:44.070 --> 00:27:49.010
of the system and in making sure that we did
everything we possibly could as a commissioning

00:27:49.010 --> 00:27:53.820
system to support those providers to deliver
the best services for patients, and where

00:27:53.820 --> 00:27:56.470
necessary, using alternative providers.

00:27:56.470 --> 00:28:03.899
I do think that my focus from two weeks ago
was absolutely operational delivery, and particularly

00:28:03.899 --> 00:28:07.100
to get the best results we could over the
Easter weekend.

00:28:07.100 --> 00:28:15.320
With certain exceptions, we had a significant
improvement of the services over that time.

00:28:15.320 --> 00:28:19.789
That operational focus will absolutely continue,
to make sure we use all the mitigation and

00:28:19.789 --> 00:28:25.700
contingency; but I think we now need to talk
as a Board about how we review the whole of

00:28:25.700 --> 00:28:26.840
the 111 service.

00:28:26.840 --> 00:28:29.639
I think we have three questions that we should
ask.

00:28:29.639 --> 00:28:35.999
Firstly, were the design and specification
of this service right, to deliver what we

00:28:35.999 --> 00:28:37.509
want for patients?

00:28:37.509 --> 00:28:40.960
Secondly, was it commissioned appropriately?

00:28:40.960 --> 00:28:43.399
Was it was commissioned to specification?

00:28:43.399 --> 00:28:46.409
Was there more that could have been done on
a national rather than local level?

00:28:46.409 --> 00:28:51.000
There are many benefits to local commissioning
of these services, but we need to think about

00:28:51.000 --> 00:28:52.240
support.

00:28:52.240 --> 00:28:58.500
Then finally, which providers did not deliver
the service that was commissioned, and what

00:28:58.500 --> 00:29:04.330
we do about those providers if there is any
suggestion that they are not going to be able

00:29:04.330 --> 00:29:05.559
to recover that provision quickly.

00:29:05.559 --> 00:29:10.250
I see that as the next phase of what NHS England
needs to do urgently.

00:29:10.250 --> 00:29:14.000
As I say, it is certainly not as the provider
of service, it is not even as the commission

00:29:14.000 --> 00:29:20.590
of service; it is in our role in supporting
CCGs to commission their services for their

00:29:20.590 --> 00:29:21.590
patients.

00:29:21.590 --> 00:29:30.790
Naguib Kheraj: Who originally designed this
new setup and authorised the transfer of it?

00:29:30.790 --> 00:29:32.420
Was that NHS England or somebody else?

00:29:32.420 --> 00:29:36.320
Barbara Hakin: The design of 111 has been
many years in the making.

00:29:36.320 --> 00:29:46.429
The phased roll-out, the decision that PCTs
should be the local commissioners that should

00:29:46.429 --> 00:29:53.249
transfer to CCGs, and the specification for
the service, were concluded before NHS England

00:29:53.249 --> 00:29:54.249
took this over.

00:29:54.249 --> 00:29:57.690
Malcolm Grant: I am conscious that time is
running against us.

00:29:57.690 --> 00:30:02.360
We can all be clear that 111 will come back
to the Board.

00:30:02.360 --> 00:30:06.270
I do not think there are just lessons learned
from how it has been handled, but it is how

00:30:06.270 --> 00:30:08.049
we handle this in the future.

00:30:08.049 --> 00:30:12.860
I think it is a unique opportunity to do something
that is not to be found elsewhere in the world

00:30:12.860 --> 00:30:17.000
that can only be done through a National Health
Service, but which needs to be done rather

00:30:17.000 --> 00:30:19.399
better than it has been done so far.

00:30:19.399 --> 00:30:23.620
Let me now turn to Leeds and invite Bruce
to respond to the points that have been raised.

00:30:23.620 --> 00:30:26.889
Bruce Keogh: Thank you very much.

00:30:26.889 --> 00:30:33.769
At the time that I had discussion with Leeds,
I was both NHS Medical Director and National

00:30:33.769 --> 00:30:37.929
Medical Director for NHS England.

00:30:37.929 --> 00:30:44.580
By the end of the long weekend, when the deliberations
had happened, I was the National Medical Director

00:30:44.580 --> 00:30:49.019
for NHS England, so there was a period of
transition.

00:30:49.019 --> 00:30:55.230
I would like to be absolutely clear that it
was the Trust that made this decision.

00:30:55.230 --> 00:31:02.080
I had no authority in either role to force
a decision in one direction or another.

00:31:02.080 --> 00:31:06.230
When I sat down and held up the mirror to
them, they will have been aware that I was

00:31:06.230 --> 00:31:10.639
Medical Director of the Commissioning Board,
they will have been aware that I was a cardiac

00:31:10.639 --> 00:31:18.029
surgeon with a national and international
track record in outcome assessment and quality

00:31:18.029 --> 00:31:21.870
measurement in heart surgery.

00:31:21.870 --> 00:31:28.720
They will also have been aware that my only
interest was in the safety of children, and

00:31:28.720 --> 00:31:33.009
that there was no other interest in my mind.

00:31:33.009 --> 00:31:37.630
Naguib Kheraj: Is it a direct commissioned
service?

00:31:37.630 --> 00:31:39.889
Bruce Keogh: It is a direct commissioned service
now, yes.

00:31:39.889 --> 00:31:41.039
Malcolm Grant: Ian?

00:31:41.039 --> 00:31:46.749
Ian Dalton: It is just worth me adding to
that that while I was coordinating the operational

00:31:46.749 --> 00:31:54.450
side of the risk machinery, I did write on
behalf of NHS England to the Trust on three

00:31:54.450 --> 00:32:00.649
occasions over the period in question, setting
out our position both as a commissioner of

00:32:00.649 --> 00:32:09.330
this service, but also to summarise the actions
of the risk summit machinery that we had operated

00:32:09.330 --> 00:32:14.710
under the National Quality Board model that
Bruce described.

00:32:14.710 --> 00:32:18.740
I think we were engaged in this in the way
that we would expect to be engaged in such

00:32:18.740 --> 00:32:23.789
issues going forward, both as a commissioner
but also as a body that has an interest in

00:32:23.789 --> 00:32:27.759
bringing the system together where concerns
have been raised through our regional and

00:32:27.759 --> 00:32:33.269
area teams, and the medical and clinical experts
within those, to understand risk then to set

00:32:33.269 --> 00:32:35.730
it out in a way that it can be addressed.

00:32:35.730 --> 00:32:37.850
Malcolm Grant: Margaret?

00:32:37.850 --> 00:32:44.879
Margaret Casely-Hayford: I would just like
to know whether the risk summit make-up included

00:32:44.879 --> 00:32:53.149
an element that would actually give the right
communication management for relatives of

00:32:53.149 --> 00:32:54.149
the patients.

00:32:54.149 --> 00:33:02.240
The feeling that I got from the way in which
the messages were coming out over the last

00:33:02.240 --> 00:33:09.750
couple of weeks was that patients’ families
felt that they did not really know what the

00:33:09.750 --> 00:33:22.460
rationale of the pause was, albeit that it
seems a very sensible and cautious move.

00:33:22.460 --> 00:33:29.840
The risk summit handled it appropriately given
the data that they had received.

00:33:29.840 --> 00:33:30.840
We have to focus now not just on listening
to patients, but also on informing them.

00:33:30.840 --> 00:33:34.149
I am not sure that that latter part has really
given the information in the way that would

00:33:34.149 --> 00:33:37.769
give the necessary comfort.

00:33:37.769 --> 00:33:45.919
Bruce Keogh: Do you want to answer that, Ian?

00:33:45.919 --> 00:33:54.169
Ian Dalton: I think we were attempting in
what was obviously a pretty rapid phase to

00:33:54.169 --> 00:33:57.080
try to communicate clearly our position.

00:33:57.080 --> 00:34:05.559
We did try to put press releases out; we have
attended meetings in Leeds to discuss the

00:34:05.559 --> 00:34:08.480
issues as they have been emerging.

00:34:08.480 --> 00:34:11.690
One of the things that we might want to do
then in the light of the points that have

00:34:11.690 --> 00:34:16.360
been made about reviewing processes is to
have a look at that issue, because I do think

00:34:16.360 --> 00:34:21.360
communicating what are often fast-moving but
at the same time really quite complicated

00:34:21.360 --> 00:34:28.740
and often data-driven driven issues in these
circumstances, that touch real people, is

00:34:28.740 --> 00:34:34.020
something that we really need to take seriously.

00:34:34.020 --> 00:34:37.820
We put a lot of effort into that, and a lot
of work was done on communications; but I

00:34:37.820 --> 00:34:41.910
think if we are looking at processes, it would
probably be worth looking at that specifically.

00:34:41.910 --> 00:34:43.070
Margaret Casely-Hayford: Thank you.

00:34:43.070 --> 00:34:44.230
Malcolm Grant: Thank you.

00:34:44.230 --> 00:34:47.390
Margaret, I think you had another question
related to something else?

00:34:47.390 --> 00:34:50.210
Margaret Casely-Hayford: Yes, that was my
additional question.

00:34:50.210 --> 00:34:51.310
Malcolm Grant: That is it?

00:34:51.310 --> 00:34:52.310
Margaret Casely-Hayford: Thank you.

00:34:52.310 --> 00:34:53.310
Malcolm Grant: Victor?

00:34:53.310 --> 00:34:55.929
Victor Adebowale: I was just thinking about
this in relation to the work that we want

00:34:55.929 --> 00:35:02.170
to do on making outcome data for surgeons
more transparent.

00:35:02.170 --> 00:35:10.820
I am wondering what the implications of doing
that will be in the light of the Leeds episode,

00:35:10.820 --> 00:35:17.930
and what we might learn from the Leeds episode
that might help us when we do make surgery

00:35:17.930 --> 00:35:19.500
outcomes more apparent.

00:35:19.500 --> 00:35:23.250
The other question I was going to ask, which
is related, is more about the NHS constitution

00:35:23.250 --> 00:35:28.890
point that David made quite some time ago
now.

00:35:28.890 --> 00:35:33.480
The NHS constitution is a good thing, but
it is only as good as people are aware of

00:35:33.480 --> 00:35:34.480
it.

00:35:34.480 --> 00:35:43.250
I just wonder how we are going to make it
a clearly breathed-in set of principles that

00:35:43.250 --> 00:35:45.410
staff and the public know about.

00:35:45.410 --> 00:35:52.940
It could be just me; my sense is that that
has not landed yet, and maybe there is a plan

00:35:52.940 --> 00:35:54.580
to test it and do it.

00:35:54.580 --> 00:36:01.350
It is only going to be as good as the first
time somebody does not do it, then the question

00:36:01.350 --> 00:36:02.880
will be, ‘What happens?’

00:36:02.880 --> 00:36:11.880
I would worry about nothing happening, and
therefore destroying the good work of the

00:36:11.880 --> 00:36:12.880
constitution.

00:36:12.880 --> 00:36:17.220
I am really keen on how we are going to make
it live.

00:36:17.220 --> 00:36:22.320
Malcolm Grant: Shall I ask Bruce to address
the first question on outcomes data?

00:36:22.320 --> 00:36:23.410
Victor Adebowale: Yes.

00:36:23.410 --> 00:36:30.120
Bruce Keogh: One of the things about publishing
data to an individual-clinician level is that

00:36:30.120 --> 00:36:37.890
it forces people to think about their outcomes
and all the things that will lead to good

00:36:37.890 --> 00:36:38.890
outcomes.

00:36:38.890 --> 00:36:48.060
It is that reflection that it introduces into
an individual’s practice that is so important.

00:36:48.060 --> 00:36:56.150
The issue with paediatric cardiac surgery
is quite tricky, because there are about 150

00:36:56.150 --> 00:37:00.870
different types of operations that are relatively
commonly done.

00:37:00.870 --> 00:37:10.530
It means that any surgeon is doing relatively
small numbers of different mixes of operations,

00:37:10.530 --> 00:37:14.670
and because of the complexity, it is quite
difficult to compare apples with apples and

00:37:14.670 --> 00:37:17.750
oranges with oranges unless you have big enough
numbers.

00:37:17.750 --> 00:37:25.630
My sense is what is really helpful is to publish
the data on children’s heart surgery at

00:37:25.630 --> 00:37:31.140
a unit level, which we have done for some
time.

00:37:31.140 --> 00:37:40.270
What has been new in this whole process is
that we now have a way of measuring and comparing

00:37:40.270 --> 00:37:44.730
outcomes that has been developed in this country
and is world-leading, which I think enables

00:37:44.730 --> 00:38:02.990
us to compare [break in audio] it was almost
a year out of date, and that is one of the

00:38:02.990 --> 00:38:06.920
reasons why we had to conduct a review in
the Unit, to see what had happened in the

00:38:06.920 --> 00:38:08.570
immediate past.

00:38:08.570 --> 00:38:14.840
We have now given every children’s heart
surgery unit some new software to enable them

00:38:14.840 --> 00:38:21.590
to compare their risk-adjusted results and
see how their results perform on a day-by-day

00:38:21.590 --> 00:38:22.590
basis.

00:38:22.590 --> 00:38:25.230
Victor Adebowale: It relates to Margaret’s
point in a way.

00:38:25.230 --> 00:38:30.410
If it were my kids, I would want somebody
to explain to me which were the best units

00:38:30.410 --> 00:38:33.540
in the country for delivering the kind of
surgery that my kids wanted, and I would want

00:38:33.540 --> 00:38:34.540
to go there.

00:38:34.540 --> 00:38:39.120
I would not really care whether it was Leeds,
Newcastle or anywhere else.

00:38:39.120 --> 00:38:42.360
I guess that is the point, really.

00:38:42.360 --> 00:38:50.000
My other point is, are we expected as a Board
to endorse the actions that we have taken?

00:38:50.000 --> 00:38:57.260
I know what has happened has happened, but
is the role of this Board to say we back Bruce

00:38:57.260 --> 00:39:00.690
– or indeed not – or is there something
else?

00:39:00.690 --> 00:39:02.950
Malcolm Grant: Can I come to that when I summarise?

00:39:02.950 --> 00:39:04.380
Victor Adebowale: Right, sorry.

00:39:04.380 --> 00:39:05.810
Malcolm Grant: Thank you.

00:39:05.810 --> 00:39:09.150
There was a point about the NHS constitution
that you raised, Victor.

00:39:09.150 --> 00:39:15.200
Moira Gibb: Before that, can I just follow
up, because I think it is a theme in a sense

00:39:15.200 --> 00:39:16.340
about accountability.

00:39:16.340 --> 00:39:21.050
Bruce has just described the information as
being inadequate, really; world-leading in

00:39:21.050 --> 00:39:28.470
one sense, but the actual individual information
that you were dealing with was a year out

00:39:28.470 --> 00:39:30.060
of date.

00:39:30.060 --> 00:39:36.450
Who is responsible in this more complex system
that we are now going to work in for making

00:39:36.450 --> 00:39:39.010
sure that is not the case in the future, as
it were?

00:39:39.010 --> 00:39:44.920
Where is the driver on the information front
about it being timely and relevant?

00:39:44.920 --> 00:39:51.330
Bruce Keogh: We are working on that, because
this is not just an issue that pertains to

00:39:51.330 --> 00:39:57.140
children’s heart surgery; it is an issue
that pervades many aspects of data in the

00:39:57.140 --> 00:39:58.320
NHS.

00:39:58.320 --> 00:40:03.750
Sticking with the children’s heart surgery
as an example, the first thing is, I think

00:40:03.750 --> 00:40:08.910
I have made it quite clear in my previous
remarks that it is up to individuals and organisations

00:40:08.910 --> 00:40:13.260
to be able to describe what they do and define
how well they do it, and that means collecting

00:40:13.260 --> 00:40:20.630
appropriate, reliable information and submitting
it to the National Clinical Audits in a timely

00:40:20.630 --> 00:40:21.630
way.

00:40:21.630 --> 00:40:27.230
I think there is a responsibility on those
who deliver services to collect appropriate

00:40:27.230 --> 00:40:30.900
information, to submit it to wherever it is
meant to be submitted.

00:40:30.900 --> 00:40:37.550
We at NHS England now have responsibility
for the National Clinical Audits, and we are

00:40:37.550 --> 00:40:42.170
already in discussion with them about how
to make this more timely.

00:40:42.170 --> 00:40:44.870
Malcolm Grant: Thank you.

00:40:44.870 --> 00:40:46.230
NHS constitution?

00:40:46.230 --> 00:40:50.530
David Nicholson: You are absolutely right,
Victor, about the NHS constitution.

00:40:50.530 --> 00:40:56.510
I should say that literally tens of thousands
of members of staff and patients were involved

00:40:56.510 --> 00:40:59.950
in the development in the first place; it
did not spring out of nothing.

00:40:59.950 --> 00:41:01.680
There has been quite a lot of work.

00:41:01.680 --> 00:41:08.410
We did quite a lot of work when we reviewed
the constitution about, in a sense, how many

00:41:08.410 --> 00:41:13.790
people knew about it, both patients and staff;
and it was not as many as you would want.

00:41:13.790 --> 00:41:19.860
We will bringing proposals to the Board some
time in the future about how we are going

00:41:19.860 --> 00:41:25.940
to lead that work – the campaigning or whatever
it is that needs to be done – to ensure

00:41:25.940 --> 00:41:33.160
that all patients understand what their rights
and responsibilities are in relation to the

00:41:33.160 --> 00:41:34.200
constitution.

00:41:34.200 --> 00:41:38.910
I think it will be something that we will
need to come back to on a number of occasions.

00:41:38.910 --> 00:41:39.910
Malcolm Grant: Thank you.

00:41:39.910 --> 00:41:48.130
I think the same will be true of the experience
at Leeds as an exemplar of the way in which

00:41:48.130 --> 00:41:52.110
we would approach a similar set of circumstances
in the future.

00:41:52.110 --> 00:41:59.170
It strikes me that the critical approach of
NHS England is that this is around outcomes

00:41:59.170 --> 00:42:01.010
for patients.

00:42:01.010 --> 00:42:04.320
That is our obsession; we will turn to that
in the next two papers that we are going to

00:42:04.320 --> 00:42:06.670
be looking at this morning.

00:42:06.670 --> 00:42:12.720
Secondly, outcomes can be measured by data,
but it is always a mistake to become obsessed

00:42:12.720 --> 00:42:14.410
by data.

00:42:14.410 --> 00:42:18.540
Data are by definition historic; they may
convey very little about what is actually

00:42:18.540 --> 00:42:21.710
happening at a particular time in a particular
unit.

00:42:21.710 --> 00:42:28.400
I believe it is important that the National
Medical Director is not only looking at evidence

00:42:28.400 --> 00:42:34.880
derived from data, but other sorts of evidence
such as whistleblowing and soft intelligence;

00:42:34.880 --> 00:42:43.100
the very factors that failed to penetrate
the carapace of officialdom in Mid Staffs.

00:42:43.100 --> 00:42:47.680
I think that those are critical indicators
of something going wrong.

00:42:47.680 --> 00:42:53.090
Thirdly, to come to the point of remit and
responsibility.

00:42:53.090 --> 00:42:59.800
As Bruce has made clear, his relationship
with Leeds was one of showing a mirror to

00:42:59.800 --> 00:43:05.480
them, as it were, as to the consequences of
the data that they themselves had submitted

00:43:05.480 --> 00:43:09.680
and which demonstrated an outlier effect against
the other units.

00:43:09.680 --> 00:43:15.140
It was the decision of Leeds themselves to
take a pause, and a decision of Leeds on further

00:43:15.140 --> 00:43:22.020
advice over the weekend to resume surgery,
albeit with certain constraints.

00:43:22.020 --> 00:43:30.300
It does seem to me that it is absolutely critical
that the stance of NHS England is precautionary.

00:43:30.300 --> 00:43:37.590
Nothing is lost by being precautionary, and
much can be gained if it is handled appropriately.

00:43:37.590 --> 00:43:43.950
However, we are conscious that this sort of
intervention occurs within an arena that is

00:43:43.950 --> 00:43:48.140
enormously politically and emotionally charged.

00:43:48.140 --> 00:43:51.920
Patients have a great deal at stake; providers
have a great deal at stake.

00:43:51.920 --> 00:43:58.440
That brings me to the final point of how important
it is to be clear about the role of NHS England,

00:43:58.440 --> 00:44:04.590
and to ensure that patients are well informed
about the reasons for and the extent of the

00:44:04.590 --> 00:44:05.590
intervention.

00:44:05.590 --> 00:44:11.220
I think absolute clarity and reassurance are
needed in everything that we do.

00:44:11.220 --> 00:44:15.640
We will have a lessons-learned; we need to
learn from this as to how we would respond

00:44:15.640 --> 00:44:22.450
in similar circumstances elsewhere, and to
be absolutely clear about process and the

00:44:22.450 --> 00:44:29.480
openness and transparency of the operation.

